# Comparing solvers

Solver choice affects both how quickly the objective improves and which solution
a run approaches. These comparisons run Basin's optimizers from shared, randomly
sampled starting points and track the objective gap $f(\mathbf{x}) - f^\star$
against elapsed time.

Each run has a {formatDuration(data.budgetNs)} time budget and stops early on
reaching a gap of $10^{-10}$. The summaries report how often each solver reaches
that target and how long the successful runs take. Expand the starting-point
figures to inspect every run. For derivative and constraint requirements, read
the [solver selection guide](../../docs/solvers/).

{#each BY_PROBLEM as group (group.problem)}
<section aria-labelledby={group.problem}>
<h2 id={group.problem}>{group.label}</h2>

This case has {group.n} parameters, with starting points sampled uniformly from
$[-2, 2]^n$. Every solver uses the same coordinates for a given seed, so
differences within a panel reflect the solver's behavior from that start.

{#if group.problem === rosenbrock}

Rosenbrock's curved valley tests how an algorithm follows changing curvature.
In this snapshot, BFGS and L-BFGS reach the target in tens of microseconds when
successful, while several algorithms plateau at a positive objective gap on some
starts. Fast progress on one start therefore needs to be read alongside target
attainment across all starts.

{/if}

<SolverResults {group} />

</section>
{/each}

## Methodology and reproduction

Measured {data.generatedAt} on {data.env.cpu} ({data.env.os}/{data.env.arch}).
Every solver uses `Vec<f64>` and the same {formatDuration(data.budgetNs)} budget
per starting point. Each per-iteration timestamp is the median over 11
repetitions of the same deterministic run. The objective gap is floored at
$10^{-10}$ for display, which is also the target used in the summary.

Absolute times are machine-specific. These inexpensive test objectives also
make solver implementation costs more visible than they would be when each
evaluation takes substantial time. Use the
[library comparisons](../competitors/) to compare
implementations of an algorithm, or the
[backend comparisons](../backends/) to examine vector
and matrix representations.

From the repository root, run the harness and collect its output:

```sh
mkdir -p target
cargo run -p competitor-bench --release --bin solver_compare > target/solver-traces.json
pnpm --dir web collect:solvers
```

The [solver harness](https://github.com/jolars/basin/blob/main/crates/competitor-bench/src/bin/solver_compare.rs)
defines the starts and configurations. The
[collection script](https://github.com/jolars/basin/blob/main/web/scripts/collect-solvers.ts)
saves the traces and machine metadata used by this page. You can also explore
the algorithms in the [interactive visualizer](../../visualizer/).
